@startuml
title TLS Handshake - Explained for a Java Developer

participant Client
participant Server

' Think of it like this:
' - Server has a KeyStore (private key + its own certificate)
' - Client has a TrustStore (list of CA certificates it trusts)
' This is exactly what SSLContext / KeyManager / TrustManager do in Java.

== Step 1: Say hello, agree on how to talk ==

Client -> Server: "Hi, here are the TLS versions/ciphers I support"
Server -> Client: "OK, let's use this version + cipher"
' Same idea as an API version negotiation

== Step 2: Server shows its ID (certificate) ==

Server -> Client: Sends its Certificate\n(contains Server's PUBLIC key)

note right of Client
Client checks the certificate, like checking an ID card:
- Was it signed by a CA I trust? (chain leads to my TrustStore)
- Is it still valid (not expired)?
- Does the domain name match?

This is exactly what Java's TrustManager
does automatically under the hood.
end note

== Step 3: Server proves it really owns that certificate ==

Server -> Client: Sends a message SIGNED with its PRIVATE key

Client -> Client: Verifies the signature using\nthe PUBLIC key from the certificate

note right of Client
Same logic as:
  publicKey.verify(signature) == true

Only the real owner of the private key
could produce a valid signature.
This is how the client knows it's
really talking to the right server,
not an impostor.
end note

== Step 4: Both sides agree on one shared secret (the session key) ==

Client -> Server: Sends its part of the key exchange data

note over Client, Server
Both sides independently calculate
the SAME secret value.

This shared secret becomes the SESSION KEY -
just a normal symmetric key, like an AES key
you'd use with Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding").

From here on, no more public/private key math -
everything is fast symmetric encryption.
end note

== Step 5: Confirm both sides really have the same key ==

Client -> Server: "Proof I have the key" (small encrypted message)
Server -> Client: "Proof I have the key too" (small encrypted message)

note over Client, Server
If either side computed a different key,
decrypting this message fails and the
connection is aborted here.
end note

== Step 6: Normal traffic, now encrypted ==

Client -> Server: Encrypted request (e.g. GET /api/data)
Server -> Client: Encrypted response (e.g. 200 OK + JSON body)

@enduml
